---
import OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery from "../../components/OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { shares, fileEvidence } from "../../data/fileCentre";
import { findGroupByName } from "../../data/groups";
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="Shared-folder permissions" description="Compare group membership, SMB access and NTFS folder rights for all five departments, including Finance's management exception." breadcrumbs={[{label:"Explore",href:"company/"},{label:"Files",href:"files/"},{label:"Permissions"}]}>
<section class="page-head"><div class="container">
<div class="eyebrow">Files / Access controls</div>
<h1>Who can open each folder?</h1>
<p class="lead" style="margin-top:17px">Instead of giving permissions individually to every staff account, Nova Corp uses department groups nested into resource groups. SMB and NTFS permissions remain separate security checks.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="section compact"><div class="container">
  <section class="content-section"><h2>Why does Nova Corp need an access matrix?</h2><p><strong>Problem:</strong> Moyo needs to modify IT documents, but confidential Finance files must remain restricted.</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong> Assign rights to department resource groups, then audit the two permission layers separately. The following matrix describes recorded root configuration rather than a live authorization simulator.</p></section><div class="paper" style="overflow-x:auto">
  <table class="data-table" style="min-width:690px">
   <caption>Reviewed departmental root permissions. Select a share or group for the full access path.</caption>
   <thead><tr><th scope="col">Department</th><th scope="col">Global membership group</th><th scope="col">Domain Local permission group</th><th scope="col">SMB</th><th scope="col">NTFS</th></tr></thead>
   <tbody>{shares.map(s=><tr>
      <th scope="row"><a href={base+"files/shares/"+s.id+"/"}>{s.name}</a></th>
      <td><a href={base+"groups/"+findGroupByName(s.group)?.slug+"/"}>{s.group}</a></td>
      <td><a href={base+"groups/"+findGroupByName(s.resourceGroup)?.slug+"/"}>{s.resourceGroup}</a></td>
      <td>Allow · Change</td><td>Allow · Modify</td>
   </tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="paper panel" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p class="kicker">Finance has an additional exception</p>
    <h3 style="margin:11px 0 12px">Authorized management accounts use a separate FullControl path.</h3>
    <p class="muted"><a href={base+"groups/management-users/"}>Management_Users</a> is nested into <a href={base+"groups/dl-fs-finance-fullcontrol/"}>DL_FS_Finance_FullControl</a>. That group has <strong>SMB Full</strong> and <strong>NTFS FullControl</strong> at the Finance root, unlike the regular Finance Modify access.</p>
    <a class="evidence-link" href={base+"files/shares/finance/"}>Explore Finance in context →</a>
  </div>
  <div class="paper panel" style="margin-top:21px">
  <p class="kicker">Look deeper / 100 recorded access entries</p>
  <h3 style="margin:12px 0">Inspect every share and folder access rule</h3>
  <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 18px">Browse all 27 SMB and 73 NTFS access entries with individual principals, inherited flags and propagation settings. The evidence covers selected scopes, not a recursive audit.</p>
  <a class="btn small" href={base+"files/permissions/technical/"}>Open the technical permission explorer →</a>
  </div>
  <div class="grid-main" style="margin-top:35px">
    <div>
    <h2>The five checkpoints of access</h2>
    <ol class="walkthrough">
      <li class="walk-step"><h3>Identity</h3><p>The user signs in using a domain account. This alone does not grant access to every company folder.</p></li>
      <li class="walk-step"><h3>Department membership</h3><p>The account is a direct member of a Global Security group, for example IT_Users.</p></li>
      <li class="walk-step"><h3>Resource group nesting</h3><p>The department group is nested into a Domain Local group associated with the folder.</p></li>
      <li class="walk-step"><h3>SMB share permission</h3><p>The share ACL allows the resource group to cross the network share boundary with Change or Full access.</p></li>
      <li class="walk-step"><h3>NTFS folder permission</h3><p>The actual folder ACL is evaluated too. A child folder can have additional or inherited rules that change the outcome.</p></li>
    </ol>
    </div>
    <aside><div class="sidebox"><h3>What was actually inspected?</h3><p class="muted">The detailed source records 27 SMB permission entries and 73 NTFS access entries across 11 shares and 14 reviewed folder scopes. It is not a recursive audit of all files.</p><a class="evidence-link" href={fileEvidence.technical}>Open every recorded permission ↗</a></div>
    <div class="sidebox"><h3>Real incident</h3><p class="muted">A controlled IT folder-denial test was later repaired. The restored snapshot shows inherited Modify and no remaining Moyo-specific explicit DENY.</p><a class="evidence-link" href={base+"incidents/file-permission-denial/"}>See the repair evidence ↗</a></div></aside>
  </div>
<section class="content-section"><OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery subject="incidents/file-permission-denial" heading="Recorded diagnostic evidence"/><h2>Engineering journals</h2><p><a href={base+"pdf-journals/NovaCorp_CR-009_Departmental_Shares_and_Drive_Mapping.pdf"}>CR-009 original access tests →</a> · <a href={base+"pdf-journals/Nova_Corp_CR-014_Engineering_Journal_and_Evidence.pdf"}>CR-014 controlled denial and repair →</a></p></section></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
